## 6. WORKSTREAM RULES — THE BUYER–SELLER WORKSPACE

The WorkStream is the ongoing workspace for a particular engagement.

It contains or connects to the relevant chat, messages, files, links, proposals, invoices, payment events, delivery submissions, revisions, reviews, cancellation events, disputes, and system messages supported by the existing implementation.

### 6.1 WorkStream creation

- A new Gig purchase creates a new Order and a new WorkStream.
- Accepting and funding a Project Proposal creates the relevant engagement and WorkStream.
- Submitting a Proposal alone does not create a WorkStream or Order.
- A follow-on Proposal created inside an existing WorkStream must continue in that same WorkStream, even if accepting it creates an additional Order.
- Do not create duplicate WorkStreams for the same continuation merely because another Order or Invoice is created.
- Do not identify a WorkStream solely by a permanent buyer-ID/seller-ID pair.

### 6.2 WorkStream after completion or cancellation

The WorkStream remains open after its associated Order is completed or cancelled.

The buyer and seller can continue communicating and may conduct repeat business through legitimate follow-on Proposals or other supported workflows.

A new direct Gig purchase creates a new WorkStream for that Gig. It must not automatically reopen or overwrite the previous Gig Order.

If the parties choose to continue work through a follow-on Proposal in an existing WorkStream, preserve that WorkStream.

Do not:

- Delete the WorkStream when an Order ends.
- Disable all chat after completion or cancellation.
- Prohibit new Proposals solely because previous work was completed or cancelled.
- Force every follow-on engagement into a completely unrelated workspace.
- Reopen or overwrite a previous completed/cancelled Order simply to represent new work.
- Prevent a legitimate new Order from being created where the existing workflow requires one.

### 6.3 WorkStream authorization

Only authorized participants and appropriately authorized administrative actors may access or mutate protected WorkStream data.

Check participant ownership and role on the server. Do not rely only on hidden frontend buttons.

A security fix must prevent unauthorized access or modification without disabling normal participant messaging, legitimate file/reference sharing, or permitted post-completion/post-cancellation communication.

### 6.4 Deliverable acceptance

The deliverable may be represented by any one of the following:

- A non-empty file.
- Non-empty URL/reference text.
- A non-empty written message.

Reject the submission as empty only when the file, URL/reference text, and written message are all blank or whitespace-only.

Do not introduce URL reachability checks, domain whitelists, URL verification, clickable-link requirements, or “fake URL” rejection rules. URLs and references may remain plain text under the existing behavior.

Continue to enforce legitimate existing upload limits and access controls.

