## 7. PROPOSALS, INVOICES, AND FOLLOW-ON WORK

### 7.1 Proposals

Proposals are offers of work and must remain distinct from Orders and payment events.

A Project Proposal does not create an Order or WorkStream until the buyer accepts it and the required funding workflow succeeds.

A follow-on Proposal sent inside an existing WorkStream continues in that WorkStream. If accepted, it may create a new Order or engagement as required by the existing implementation without duplicating the WorkStream.

A seller may propose further work after a prior Order has completed or been cancelled, provided the existing workflow permits the proposal.

A buyer may accept a legitimate Proposal and fund the new engagement under the existing rules.

Do not prohibit proposals simply because an earlier Order between the same parties exists.

### 7.2 Invoices

Invoices are financial/workflow records associated with the appropriate engagement and WorkStream.

An Invoice must accurately reflect its status, such as Sent, Paid, or Cancelled, according to the existing model.

Where the existing UI provides buyer accept/reject actions, keep those actions on the relevant existing card. Where the existing UI provides seller cancellation and resending actions, preserve them.

Do not duplicate cards, create redundant payment flows, or charge the buyer again merely because an invoice is sent or accepted.

For a funded Gig, the final invoice/submission is the formal completion/payment-request event. It releases the existing escrow only when the applicable approval/release conditions are satisfied.

For other engagement types, preserve their existing legitimate funding and invoice semantics rather than blindly reusing Gig logic.

