## 9. CANCELLATIONS, LATE ORDERS, REVISIONS, AND DISPUTES

Use the existing marketplace cancellation and dispute workflows. Do not invent a different policy while fixing technical defects.

### 9.1 Cancellation

Preserve the existing Fiverr-style cancellation reasons and the current buyer/seller cancellation experience.

Do not invent new cancellation eligibility requirements or silently change who can request or respond to cancellation.

A cancellation request is a workflow state, not proof that a refund has already succeeded.

An open cancellation request must block automatic completion/release where the established rules require that protection.

An Order should become accepted/auto-cancelled only after the required refund operation succeeds. If the refund fails, do not falsely present the cancellation as financially completed; preserve a recoverable state.

If the buyer rejects a seller's cancellation request, return the Order to the appropriate delivered/review state and start the established fresh three-day review window.

Do not impose a permanent “one cancellation request per delivery” rule. After a rejection, another cancellation request may be allowed if it is otherwise eligible under the existing policy.

Do not make expired requests permanently block the Order or prevent legitimate responses and recovery.

### 9.2 Late-order protection

The established late-order protection period is 48 hours after the delivery deadline.

When a cancellation/rejection outcome occurs while the Order remains late, the existing late-order protection behavior may restart the 48-hour period as specified by the current workflow.

Do not reintroduce the old 76-hour Gig refund rule.

Do not add unrelated waiting periods or alter the existing late-order policy.

### 9.3 Seller cancellation response

The seller cancellation request's buyer response period is 48 hours under the established rule.

The system must handle expiry and automatic outcomes consistently with the existing cancellation/refund workflow.

A timeout must not create a refund twice, silently corrupt Order state, or leave the user without a valid resolution path.

### 9.4 Revisions

Preserve the existing revision request/resolution workflow.

A valid revision must not be accidentally treated as a final approved delivery. A revision must use the applicable existing delivery/review lifecycle.

Do not prohibit legitimate revisions or add new revision limits without an explicit business-rule instruction.

### 9.5 Disputes

Disputes must be recorded consistently with the associated Order and financial state.

A disputed Order must not be automatically released through a path that ignores the dispute.

However, a dispute must not permanently freeze all financial activity regardless of its outcome. Use the existing dispute-resolution process to determine the valid next step.

Do not introduce a new dispute policy or additional blanket restrictions.

