## 12. AUTHENTICATION, AUTHORIZATION, AND PRIVACY

Security must protect the platform without changing the ordinary user experience unnecessarily.

### 12.1 Authentication

Use the existing authentication architecture.

Protect session validation, revocation, token handling, password changes, and login flows against genuine security defects.

Production deployments must not rely on demo authentication, predictable signing secrets, or development-only shortcuts.

If a production configuration is missing a security-critical setting, fail safely rather than silently enabling an insecure fallback.

Do not disable legitimate login, signup, password reset, or account recovery.

Preserve the existing three-device session limit.

### 12.2 Authorization

Every sensitive server-side action must verify the correct actor, ownership, role, and relevant state.

Do not rely exclusively on frontend checks.

Do not allow an ordinary administrator or user to promote themselves to a more privileged role, alter another user's protected credentials, or bypass intended administrative controls.

Do not impose new global restrictions on normal users as a substitute for correct role and ownership checks.

### 12.3 Public profiles and private information

Public user endpoints must expose only fields intended for public display.

Do not expose private email addresses, mobile numbers, wallet balances, earnings, internal status flags, administrative flags, or other non-public information through a public profile response.

Use the existing public-user sanitization behavior consistently across direct lookups and public profile routes.

Protect private information without disabling legitimate public profiles, Gig discovery, or permitted marketplace access.

### 12.4 WebSockets and notifications

Verify the intended recipient and authorization before delivering private messages, notifications, or WorkStream events.

Do not broadcast private data to unrelated users.

Keep authorized messaging and notification delivery functional. Do not disable WebSockets or WorkStream notifications as a shortcut for fixing recipient filtering.

### 12.5 Rate limiting and diagnostics

Preserve the established message/upload limits and existing rate-limiting behavior.

Do not rely on spoofable client-supplied identity or IP information where a trusted server-derived value is required.

Protect diagnostic logs from leaking secrets and avoid unnecessary synchronous I/O that harms production functionality.

